<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Nanda Empire</h1><p class="st-meta">345 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.) · capital Pataliputra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first empire of the Ganges valley, whose wealth and great army were feared even by Alexander's troops.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 345 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahapadma-nanda">Mahapadma Nanda</a>, said by tradition to be of low birth, overthrew the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shishunaga-dynasty">Shishunaga</a> ruler of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Kingdom of Magadha</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> call him a destroyer of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kshatriya">Kshatriya</a> dynasties, and he extended Magadhan rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and probably into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kalinga">Kalinga</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Nandas built a centralised state with a large standing army; Greek writers reported 200,000 infantry, 20,000 cavalry, thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. They were famous for their treasury, filled by heavy taxes, and for public works such as canals. Their fearsome reputation helped convince Alexander's soldiers to refuse to march beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a> in 326 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> and Buddhist sources alike remember the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhana-nanda">Dhana Nanda</a>, as greedy and unpopular.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 322 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-maurya">Chandragupta Maurya</a>, guided according to tradition by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chanakya">Chanakya</a>, overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhana-nanda">Dhana Nanda</a> and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, which inherited the Nanda administration and capital.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nanda-empire.jpg" alt="Nanda Empire" width="320" height="299"><figcaption>Image: Avantiputra7 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanda_Empire,_c.325_BCE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>345 BCE – 322 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pataliputra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahapadma Nanda, Dhana Nanda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion, Jainism, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Magadhi Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>, <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nanda dynasty, Nandas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-334&amp;focus=nanda-empire" aria-label="Nanda Empire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6933 1236 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nanda Empire lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7253 1492L7253 1492M7253 1492L7034 1363M7253 1492L7027 1443M7253 1492L7266 1513M7253 1492L7040 1426M7253 1492L7305 1688"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7253 1492h0M7034 1363h0M7027 1443h0M7266 1513h0M7040 1426h0M7305 1688h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7253 1492h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pataliputra. <a href="/atlas?year=-334&amp;focus=nanda-empire">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
